From 3d92e777e4146aab96af58a9d041d8d138c19669 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 21:14:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer: what is decided about a video of two views
 (H.264 MVC)

A 3D Blu-ray, and a Matroska file made of one, code the picture of each
eye as a view of one H.264 stream (MVC): a base view that any decoder
plays, and a second view that is predicted from it. Kodi plays the base
view of such a stream, and nothing tells it of the other one.

This is the part of playing both that is decided rather than done, with
nothing of FFmpeg or of Kodi in it, so that it can be tested by itself
(KODI::MULTIVIEW, MultiviewChoice.h):

- whether the private data of the codec of a track declares a second
  view. The record of the base view ('avcC') is walked to the end of
  its parameter sets, and behind it a box 'mvcC' has to lie within the
  data and hold a subset sequence parameter set of the Multiview High
  or the Stereo High profile. The four letters alone, which could stand
  anywhere, do not count;
- which eye the base view is, by what the container says of the track
  ("both eyes in one block", the left or the right one first, or
  nothing at all), and that a container which names a layout within
  the picture describes another stream;
- which two pictures of the decoder are the two views of one access
  unit: they are named as different views and have the same
  presentation time and the same size. Two pictures without a time are
  not known to belong together, however they follow each other. The
  order in which the two views come is not relied upon;
- what is done with a picture whose other view does not come: a base
  view goes out alone, to both eyes, and a second view is never shown;
- the picture that holds both views side by side: twice as wide, with
  pixels half as wide, so that it has the shape of one view, which is
  what a picture with both eyes side by side is taken to be;
- when the second view of a stream is given up: when it never came in
  ten seconds of film, or when the decoder takes nothing.

Nothing uses it yet.

Finding such a track by its 'mvcC' box and giving the renderer one
picture with both eyes side by side is what Dom Cobley (popcornmix)
does in his Kodi branch fix-stereoscopic-3d-gbm-upstream; the code
here is written anew.

Checked with a host test (plain C++20, with the address and the
undefined behaviour sanitizers), 224 checks. Among them: every cut of a
private data that holds, which never holds and is never read beyond its
end; and every stream of up to seven pictures made of two views, three
times, a picture without a time and one without the name of its view
(2 396 744 streams), in which two pictures of different access units
never go out as one and every base view goes out exactly once, in its
order. (By the time and the name that each picture carries: what the
decoder says of its pictures is taken to be true.)
